ITEMS = ((('what is shock in first aid', 'what should i do for someone in shock', 'how do i treat shock', 'what is the first aid for shock', 'how to help a person with shock', 'what does shock mean after an injury', 'how do you handle shock in a casualty', 'what helps someone in shock in shock', 'how can i respond to shock', 'what is the best action for shock'), 'Shock is a life-threatening state where the body cannot circulate blood properly. Call emergency services, lay the person down, keep them warm and treat any bleeding. Do not give food or drink, and stay with them until help arrives.'), (('what are the signs of shock', 'how do i recognise shock in someone', 'what does a person in shock look like', 'which symptoms suggest shock', 'how to spot shock after bleeding', 'what if skin is pale and clammy in shock', 'how to check for shock in a casualty', 'what breathing changes occur in shock', 'how to tell if someone is going into shock', 'what signs of shock need urgent help'), 'Signs of shock include pale or grey skin, cold clammy hands, rapid breathing, weakness, thirst and confusion. The person may seem anxious or very still. Call emergency services immediately if you notice these signs.'), (('should i lay a person down for shock', 'what position helps someone in shock', 'should i raise the legs in shock', 'how to position a casualty with shock', 'what if lying flat makes breathing worse in shock', 'should i sit someone up in shock', 'how to keep a shock victim still', 'what if moving could worsen an injury in shock', 'how to support the body during shock', 'who can advise on positioning for shock'), 'Lay the person flat and keep them still unless another injury makes that unsafe. Raising the legs may help if it does not cause pain, but do not force it. Call emergency services and follow their advice if breathing is affected.'), (('how do i keep someone warm during shock', 'should i cover a person in shock', 'what if the person is shivering in shock', 'can i use a coat for warmth in shock', 'should i give a hot drink for shock', 'how to insulate someone from the ground in shock', 'what if clothing is wet in shock', 'how to warm a casualty safely in shock', 'when to ask about warming in shock', 'who takes over warming after shock'), 'Cover the person with a coat or blanket and insulate them from the ground. Do not use direct heat such as a hot water bottle, and do not give hot drinks while they are unwell. Call emergency services and keep checking their breathing.'), (('can severe bleeding cause shock', 'how to prevent shock from bleeding', 'what to do for bleeding and shock', 'should i control bleeding first in shock', 'what if blood loss leads to shock', 'how to watch for shock after a wound', 'what to do while waiting with bleeding in shock', 'when to call help for shock from bleeding', 'how to keep pressure on a wound in shock', 'who treats shock after blood loss'), 'Severe bleeding can quickly lead to shock, so apply firm pressure to the wound and call emergency services. Keep the person still and warm while waiting. Tell the crew how much bleeding you saw and what first aid you gave.'), (('can burns lead to shock', 'what to watch for after a bad burn in shock', 'how to care for someone in shock from burns', 'should i cool a burn if shock is suspected', 'what if a burn covers a large area in shock', 'how to keep a burned person warm in shock', 'when to call emergency services for burns and shock', 'what signs of shock follow burns', 'how to cover burns while waiting in shock', 'who assesses shock after burns'), 'Large burns can cause shock through fluid loss. Cool the burn briefly with water, cover it loosely, keep the person warm and call emergency services. Do not give food or drink, and watch for confusion or worsening breathing.'), (('is anaphylaxis a type of shock', 'what to do for shock from an allergic reaction', 'how to recognise shock after a sting', 'what if swelling comes with shock', 'should i use an adrenaline injector for shock', 'how to help someone with allergy and shock', 'what to do if a person collapses after a reaction in shock', 'when to call emergency services for allergic shock', 'how to position someone in allergic shock', 'who can treat shock from an allergy'), 'A severe allergic reaction can cause shock with swelling, breathing difficulty and collapse. Call emergency services at once, help the person use their prescribed injector if they have one, and keep them still. Do not delay calling for help.'), (('should i give water to a person in shock', 'can i give food for shock', 'what not to give someone in shock', 'is it safe to give tablets in shock', 'why avoid drinks during shock', 'what if the person says they are thirsty in shock', 'how to care for the mouth in shock', 'when to offer fluids after shock', 'who can decide about giving drinks in shock', 'what to tell the operator about shock'), 'Do not give food, drink or tablets to someone in shock because they may choke or vomit. Keep them still and warm and call emergency services. If they become fully alert later, ask a qualified person before offering fluids.'), (('how do i recognise shock in a child', 'what to do for shock in a baby', 'are signs of shock different in children', 'what if a child is pale and drowsy in shock', 'how to keep a child warm in shock', 'when to call emergency services for child shock', 'how to position a child in shock', 'what if a child has vomiting with shock', 'how to check breathing in child shock', 'who can assess a child in shock'), "A child in shock may be pale, floppy, drowsy or breathing quickly. Call emergency services, keep them warm and still, and do not give food or drink. Check breathing often and be ready to follow the operator's instructions."), (('when should i call emergency services for shock', 'is shock always a medical emergency', 'what if shock does not improve', 'should i call for faintness after an injury in shock', 'what signs need an ambulance for shock', 'how quickly must help arrive in shock', 'what to report about shock symptoms', 'should i stay with someone in shock', 'when to start cpr during shock', 'who takes over care from me in shock'), 'Call emergency services whenever you suspect shock, especially after bleeding, burns or illness. Report the signs and what happened. Stay with the person, keep them warm and start CPR if breathing stops.'))
